  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM MST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nToday through Thu night...Closed upper level low at the \nnorthern CA coast moves onshore this afternoon and breaks down \nthe upper level ridge that will dominate Idaho weather today. \nDuring the day Wed, this low will move quickly through northern \nUT and give a slight chance threat to southern zones at that \ntime. By late Wed afternoon, it has moved out of southern Idaho,\npushed out by the next incoming impulse through the mean \nwesterly flow. This time, though, the track is through Canada. \nThe southern end of this trough and assoicated surface front \ndoes move through the northern end of the forecast area. Once \nagain, some slight chance threat for some precipitation on \nThu/Thu night, in all areas. \n\nThis constant barrage of short wave impulses after today will\nmean little change to the temperatures over eastern Idaho. Lower\nelevations will warm up today, to around the 50 degree mark,\nthen hover around that for afternoon highs through this period.\nOvernight lows will also change little during this period.\n\nWithout any serious, organized feature, wind should remain light\nto moderate with afternoon wind higher when wind aloft gets a\nchance to mix down to the surface, but nothing should be\ncritical. \n\nThe other major consideration is stratus and fog for this\nmorning. Satellite imagery does show a fairly large patch of\nstratus/fog/mist over the eastern half of the forecast area,\nthat does have some spreading out to the west. Unfortunately, a\ncirrus shield is also moving in over the top and obscuring the\nstratus/fog/mist imagery. At this time, Idaho Falls has this\nlower deck over them and has around 4000ft AGL Ceiling. VFR\nceilings like that may kill the ability to develop fog this\nmorning, at least in this eastern half. The cirrus shield may do\nthe same in the western half.",
